WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.464
The fraud check caches merchant profiles, and the cache has room for eight.

2
00:00:07.464 --> 00:00:12.228
Traffic is skewed: nine requests in ten ask for one of four busy merchants.

3
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:15.271
When it's full, a new key pushes one out.

4
00:00:15.271 --> 00:00:19.423
LRU evicts the key used least recently, the one at this end.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:21.912
A hit moves a key to the recent end.

6
00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:25.773
A miss brings the new key in there, and the oldest drops out.

7
00:00:25.773 --> 00:00:29.381
So the busy merchants stay, and nine requests in ten hit.

8
00:00:29.567 --> 00:00:37.232
Then the nightly report reads all 1,000 merchants, each one once, ten for every request the fraud check makes.

9
00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:43.669
Each report key is new, so it misses, goes in at the recent end, and pushes the oldest key out.

10
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.363
Five report keys later, the busy merchants are gone.

11
00:00:47.363 --> 00:00:50.825
The cache is full of keys nobody will ask for again.

12
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:54.709
So while the report runs, the fraud check misses every time.

13
00:00:54.709 --> 00:00:59.793
Its hits fall from nine in ten to none, and come back only when the report is done.

14
00:00:60.000 --> 00:01:04.961
SIEVE keeps the keys in the order they arrived, and gives each one a visited bit.

15
00:01:04.961 --> 00:01:07.288
A hit sets the bit, and nothing moves.

16
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:11.557
To make room, a hand walks from the oldest key toward the newest.

17
00:01:11.557 --> 00:01:16.717
A key whose bit is set gets a second chance: the hand clears the bit and moves on.

18
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.459
The first key with a clear bit is evicted.

19
00:01:19.459 --> 00:01:22.993
The new key goes in at the newest end, with its bit clear.

20
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.438
Now run the report.

21
00:01:24.438 --> 00:01:30.860
The hand clears the busy merchants' bits as it passes, and evicts the keys nobody came back for.

22
00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:33.544
Then it stops among the report keys.

23
00:01:33.544 --> 00:01:39.365
Each new report key evicts the one before it, and the hand never reaches the busy merchants.

24
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.263
Meanwhile the fraud check keeps asking for them, so their bits are set again, and they hit.

25
00:01:45.263 --> 00:01:47.159
Its hits stay near nine in ten.

26
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:50.688
A one-time scan makes every key look recent to LRU.

27
00:01:50.688 --> 00:01:55.572
SIEVE keeps the keys that were asked for again, so the scan passes through.
